%[text] # My Scuba Diving Simulator
%[text] [![](text:image:6447)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=simulink/blog&project=2026_06_20_scuba/ScubaSimulator.prj)
%[text] I recently took a week of vacation to go scuba diving in the Caribbean. In the middle of a dive, at about 80 feet down, this idea came to my mind: **I need to make a scuba diving simulator!**
%[text] I had thought about creating this simulation before, but I always ended up struggling with one thing or another. Typically, I would get stuck when trying to add the effect of depth on the diver's lungs and the [buoyancy control device (BCD)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buoyancy_compensator_(diving)).
%[text] Today, with an AI agent and the [Simulink Agentic Toolkit](https://www.mathworks.com/products/simulink-agentic-toolkit.html), I thought it was time to give this another try.
%%
%[text] ## The Plan
%[text] Everyone who has worked with AI agents will tell you: The planning part is very important!
%[text] I thought for some time about what I wanted this simulator to include and how I wanted it to be implemented. Once the picture was clear in my mind, I wrote my requirements:
%[text] - I want to simulate a typical hour-long scuba dive profile
%[text] - It should model air consumption during the dive
%[text] - It needs to model the diver's buoyancy throughout the dive
%[text] - The diver's buoyancy should be controlled by the diver's breathing and the buoyancy control device (BCD)
%[text] - The mechanical part of the simulation should use the [Simscape 1D position-based translational domain](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simscape/lang/position-based-mechanical-translational-domain.html)
%[text] - The gas part should be implemented using a simple custom gas domain with constant temperature approximation
%[text] - The depth information computed in the mechanical domain will be passed to the custom gas components that need it through a translational mechanical connection port
%[text] - The depth control logic should be implemented in Simulink and Stateflow
%[text] - Three control signals should be sent to the plant model: BCD inflate valve, BCD release valve and diver breathing effort
%[text] - The plant model should include the following individual Simscape components: Gas tank, first-stage regulator, BCD with intake and release valves, second stage regulator with intake and release valves. \
%[text] Based on those requirements, the Simulink Agentic Toolkit invoked multiple sub agents to fetch relevant information from many reliable sources. Here are some examples:
%[text] ![](text:image:26d2)
%[text] ![](text:image:6b97)
%[text] In the end, it created 4 plan files
%[text] - **scuba-diver-system.md:** A high-level description of the system, with executive summary, objectives, success criteria.
%[text] - **scuba-diver-architecture.md:** We are going into more details, listing the individual components, their interface, their equations.
%[text] - **scuba-diver-implementation-plan.md:** This went into implementation of the full simulation, including controllers for dive profile scenarios, breathing waveform and BCD Controller
%[text] - **scuba-diver-test-plan.md:** How all this will be validated. \
%[text] ## The Simscape SCUBA Library
%[text] The first implementation step I went through is the Simscape library. The agent was pretty good at generating the components I wanted. Here is what the component looked like: 
%[text] ![](text:image:5db8)
%[text] The underwater gas domain definition code is:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] domain underwaterGas
%[text] % Custom gas domain for scuba simulation.
%[text] % Across variable: pressure (Pa)
%[text] % Through variable: molar flow rate (mol/s)
%[text]     parameters
%[text]         R_gas = {8.314, 'J/(mol*K)'};  % Universal gas constant
%[text]         T     = {293.15, 'K'};         % Gas temperature (isothermal)
%[text]     end
%[text] 
%[text]     variables(Balancing = true)
%[text]         n_dot = {0, 'mol/s'};          % Molar flow rate (through)
%[text]     end
%[text] 
%[text]     variables
%[text]         p     = {101325, 'Pa'};        % Pressure (across)
%[text]     end
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] For a component like the diver lungs, the ideal gas law is used to compute the lungs volume at the current depth, and the Archimedes principle to compute the buoyancy force: 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text]     intermediates
%[text]         R_gas  = A.R_gas;
%[text]         T      = A.T;
%[text]         depth  = R.x;
%[text]         P_amb  = P_atm + rho_water * R.gravity * depth;
%[text]         F_buoy = rho_water * R.gravity * V_lungs;
%[text]     end
%[text] 
%[text]     equations
%[text]         V_lungs == V_lungs_in;
%[text]         A.p == P_lung;
%[text]         der(n_lungs) == n_dot;
%[text]         % Ideal gas law
%[text]         P_lung * V_lungs == n_lungs * R_gas * T;
%[text]         f == -F_buoy;
%[text]     end
%[text] ```
%[text] ### Generating the Simscape library using an AI agent
%[text] Here are a few items I had to iterate with the agent to get the way I wanted
%[text] #### Simscape language syntax
%[text] As of today, the Simulink Agentic Toolkit does not include guidance for the Simscape language. Because of that, the agent initially ended up using old-style syntax like the setup function and let-in-end pattern that are either obsolete or will be soon. Once I realized that, I simply prompted the agent with the following and it succeeded in seconds:
%[text] "*Update all Let-in-end pattern in Simscape language with the more modern intermediate syntax. Search the MathWorks documentation for examples*"
%[text] #### Block Icons
%[text] By default, Simscape blocks have no icon. To make my new library of blocks look better, I asked the agent to generate images. The request was something like:
%[text] "*Generate SVG image files for all components in the scuba library. Images should be representative of the block functionality. Update the "annotation" section of the* *`.ssc`* *files to reference the icon image*"
%[text] For complex components like the lungs, gas tank and BCD bladder, I went through a few iterations to refine the image.
%[text] #### Lung model
%[text] In my initial request, I specified that the lung input should be the "diver breathing effort". Testing showed that I was wrong with that. 
%[text] While inspecting the test results, I realized that when going deeper, the diver was not consuming more air as expected. Instead, the lungs were getting smaller. After a bit of research, I realized that the lung input should be volume. The agent was able to fix that easily.
%[text] At the end of the day, I did not write a single line of Simscape code myself and got the fully functional Simscape components I needed.
%[text] ## Assembling the SCUBA Diver
%[text] Next step: Assemble the plant model. Unfortunately, I have to admit that the current layout algorithm in the Simulink Agentic Toolkit struggles with Simscape and physical connections. I asked the agent to give it a first shot. It added all the blocks with the correct parameterization, and I manually moved the blocks and lines around until I got a layout I was happy with.
%[text] I grouped the components in two subsystems: mechanical and gas.
%[text] The mechanical part is mostly the diver body and weights.
%[text] ![](text:image:9a2c)
%[text] For the gas network, I used the Simscape [Connection Label](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simscape/ref/connectionlabel.html) block to share the depth information with all components.
%[text] ![](text:image:0f3f)
%[text] ## Testing
%[text] Once I had my diver assembled, I put together tests for a few simple maneuvers.
%%
%[text] ### Simple Ascent-Descent
%[text] As a first test, I put together this model.
mdl = 'descent_test';
open_system(mdl)
%[text] ![](text:image:4f9f)
%[text] At the beginning of the simulation, the BCD is empty and the diver descends. When reaching 15 meters, the BCD is inflated for some amount of time. We can see that a small fraction of a second makes the difference between sinking to the bottom and ascending back up.
mdl = 'descent_test';
inflateVector = 0.75:0.01:0.8;
N = length(inflateVector);
in = repmat(Simulink.SimulationInput(mdl),N,1);
in = arrayfun(@(i) in(i).setVariable('InflateDT',inflateVector(i)),1:N);
out = sim(in,'ShowProgress','off','UseFastRestart','on');
figure %[output:84f4f8d3]
plot(out(1).logsout.get('Desired Depth').Values) %[output:84f4f8d3]
hold on %[output:84f4f8d3]
for i = 1:N
plot(out(i).logsout.get('Depth').Values); %[output:84f4f8d3]
end
hold off %[output:84f4f8d3]
labels = compose("Inflate duration = %.2f sec", inflateVector);  % string array
legend(labels,'Location','northwest'); %[output:84f4f8d3]
xlabel('Time (sec)') %[output:84f4f8d3]
ylabel('Depth (m)') %[output:84f4f8d3]
%%
%[text] ### Air Consumption
%[text] When diving, the deeper you go, the more air you use. To validate that this is modeled properly, I used this simple harness where I "pinned" the diver at certain depths and let it breathe normally for an hour.
mdl = 'breath_test';
open_system(mdl);
%[text] ![](text:image:4126)
%[text] I can run the simulation for various depths.
breathTestDepthV = 5:5:30;
N = length(breathTestDepthV);
in = repmat(Simulink.SimulationInput(mdl),N,1);
in = arrayfun(@(i) in(i).setVariable('breathTestDepth',breathTestDepthV(i)),1:N);
out = sim(in,'ShowProgress','off','UseFastRestart','on');
%[text] The results match quite well what I experience myself while diving.
figure %[output:8b901417]
hold on %[output:8b901417]
for i = 1:N
    ts = out(i).logsout.get('Tank Pressure').Values;   % timeseries
    plot(ts.Time/60, ts.Data)                          % plot minutes vs data %[output:8b901417]
end
hold off %[output:8b901417]
labels = compose("Depth = %dm", breathTestDepthV);  % string array
legend(labels) %[output:8b901417]
xlabel('Time (min)') %[output:8b901417]
ylabel('Tank Pressure (PSI)') %[output:8b901417]
title('Air consumption at different depths'); %[output:8b901417]
%%
%[text] ## Full Control
%[text] I am still working on controlling the diver's depth for a full dive profile. It turns out that combining the BCD and breathing to control depth is more complicated than it looks like. Now that [Claude Fable](https://www.anthropic.com/claude/fable) is available, maybe I'll give it a try and see if it can figure it out.
%[text] If anyone is interested, grab my [scuba-simulator project on GitHub](https://github.com/guirlo/scuba-simulator) and send me a pull request if you can come up with a good control logic. 
%%
